{
  "title": "Hypatia (Nekomy LMS) \u2014 edtech (651\u2605)",
  "content": "Project: Hypatia (Nekomy LMS)\nDomain: edtech | Scale: MVP | Stars: 651\nProblem: Provides an open-source, JavaScript-based Learning Management System (LMS) for hosting MOOCs and online courses. It enables educators to create and manage course content with a modern SPA interface backed by a real-time database.\nTarget users: Educators and instructors creating online courses, Students and learners enrolling in MOOCs, Developers and institutions needing a self-hosted open-source LMS\nCore features: User authentication and role-based access (admin, student); Course and lesson content management with Markdown editor; Admin dashboard for platform and content management; Real-time Firebase database and storage integration; Student enrollment and progress tracking; Google Analytics integration for usage tracking; Slack integration for notifications; Emoji support and rich media in course content\nIntegrations: Firebase (Authentication, Realtime Database, Storage, Hosting), Google Analytics (react-ga), Slack API, Google Creative Commons (media)\nTech stack: React 15, Redux, React Router v2, SCSS + Firebase (BaaS \u2014 no custom backend server) + Firebase Realtime Database + Firebase Hosting, Webpack, Travis CI\nKey libraries: redux-react-firebase, redux-thunk, redux-promise, react-simplemde-editor, showdown, react-helmet, react-datepicker, lodash, moment, classnames, react-router-redux\nArchitecture: monolith\nDesign lessons:\n- Fully serverless architecture using Firebase as BaaS eliminates the need for a custom backend, simplifying deployment but creating tight vendor coupling.\n- Role-based access control is implemented at the data level (user 'level' field in Firebase) rather than a dedicated auth service, a pragmatic but limited approach for an MVP.\n- Redux is used for state management with firebase-redux binding to sync real-time database state into the Redux store, enabling reactive UI updates.\n- Airbnb ESLint configuration enforces consistent code style, indicating an emphasis on code quality despite the project being an early-stage open-source tool.",
